            {
              "id": "5ad0209e-dfa2-317a-a636-a2ea2da42354",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Found\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities as it accesses the environment variables, which can be manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` function directly. Instead, use safer alternatives that limit the size of the input and output. Validate all input and output data to ensure it meets the expected format and size. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would involve replacing the `getenv` function with a safer alternative. However, without knowing the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. Generally, you should consider using a function that limits the size of the input and output, and validates the data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to depend on the `rocsparse` library, which is a library for sparse matrix computations on AMD GPUs. \n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be available in the future. Always refer to the latest documentation and resources for the most accurate and up-to-date information."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "09dfe0ea-84cf-3dc9-8592-dafe749d555a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is part of the environment of the process. If the environment is changed (for example, by another thread), the string may be changed as well.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions whenever possible. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries. In the case of `getenv`, consider using a thread-safe and reentrant function instead, if available.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe code fix would depend on the specific requirements of your program. If you need to access environment variables, consider using a safer alternative to `getenv`. For example, you could use the `std::getenv` function from the C++ standard library, which is also thread-safe:\n\n```cpp\n(!std::getenv(rocsparse::envariables::names[rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_WARNINGS]))\n                    ? debug\n                    : ROCSPARSE_ENVARIABLES.get(rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_WARNINGS)\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to depend on the following libraries:\n\n- The C++ standard library (for `std::getenv`)\n- The `rocsparse` library (for `rocsparse::envariables::names` and `rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_WARNINGS`)\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0bf10869-c32e-3255-9ba1-67c02fc66e96",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `getenv()` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if the environment variable it retrieves is controlled by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using unsafe C functions whenever possible. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ standard library or other libraries. In the case of `getenv()`, consider using a method that validates the environment variable's value before using it, or use a configuration file or command-line arguments to pass configuration to your program.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\nconst char* env_var_value = getenv(rocsparse::envariables::names[rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_ARGUMENTS_VERBOSE]);\nif (env_var_value != nullptr) {\n    // Validate the environment variable's value before using it\n    if (isValidEnvVarValue(env_var_value)) {\n        ROCSPARSE_ENVARIABLES.get(rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_ARGUMENTS_VERBOSE);\n    } else {\n        // Handle invalid environment variable value\n    }\n} else {\n    debug_arguments;\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `isValidEnvVarValue()` is a function that you would need to implement to validate the environment variable's value.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to depend on the `rocsparse` library, which is a library for sparse matrix computations on AMD GPUs.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2bace41e-14d5-35e1-9e10-9d90665942b4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is stored in an environment variable. This can be problematic because the environment in which a program runs may be controlled or influenced by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using the `getenv` function. Instead, consider using safer alternatives that are less prone to security issues. For example, you could use a configuration file to store settings, or use command line arguments to pass settings to your program. If you must use environment variables, be sure to validate and sanitize the input to prevent injection attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `getenv` function with a safer alternative. For example, you could use the `std::getenv` function from the C++ standard library, which is considered safer than the C `getenv` function. Here is how you could modify the code:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* env_var = std::getenv(rocsparse::envariables::names[rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_VERBOSE]);\n(!env_var) ? debug : ROCSPARSE_ENVARIABLES.get(rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_VERBOSE);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example appears to depend on the `rocsparse` library, which is a library for sparse matrix computations on AMD GPUs.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not always be accessible."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "364bf417-2b99-3d90-849c-2a37aafe8f5b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory through data flow, which can cause unpredictable results when using functions like memcpy. Additionally, there is no explicit check or guard in the code to ensure that the source and destination do not overlap. The source pointer may also be null at this point, as there is no prior check to guarantee it is valid, further increasing the risk. The destination is confirmed to be non-null, but this does not mitigate the overlap or null source risks. These factors together make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the use of `memcpy` with `memmove`, which is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. For example, change the code to:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(col.data(), csr_col_ind_C, sizeof(J) * nnz);\n```\n\nBefore performing the copy, also ensure that the source pointer (`csr_col_ind_C`) is not null to avoid a potential null pointer dereference:\n\n```cpp\nif (csr_col_ind_C != nullptr) {\n    memmove(col.data(), csr_col_ind_C, sizeof(J) * nnz);\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap, and avoids undefined behavior due to a null source pointer.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "3372baf0-56b8-3b7c-b5ca-354533621e19",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The analysis detected that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory areas, which can cause unpredictable results with functions like memcpy. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit bounds or capacity checks further increases the risk. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when the source and destination memory regions may overlap, replace the memory copy operation with a function that safely handles overlapping regions, such as `memmove`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null before performing the operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (val.data() != nullptr && csr_val_C != nullptr) {\n    memmove(val.data(), csr_val_C, sizeof(T) * nnz);\n}\n```\nThis change ensures that the copy is safe even if the memory regions overlap, and avoids null pointer dereference. Always validate the size calculation to ensure it does not exceed the allocated buffer size.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c7f292c6-5aad-3ded-abca-3a305955ba3f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In this case, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities if not used carefully. The `getenv` function returns a pointer to a string that is part of the environment of the process. If the environment is changed (by another thread or by a function called by the thread), the string may be overwritten.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `getenv` function directly. Instead, use safer alternatives that are less prone to misuse, such as `getenv_s` in Windows or `secure_getenv` in Linux. If these are not available, consider copying the returned string to a buffer controlled by your application immediately after calling `getenv`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* env_var = getenv(rocsparse::envariables::names[rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG_ARGUMENTS]);\nstd::string env_var_copy = env_var ? env_var : \"\";\n(!env_var_copy.empty()) ? debug : ROCSPARSE_ENVARIABLES.get(rocsparse::envariables::DEBUG);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example seems to depend on the `rocsparse` library, which is a part of the ROCm software platform. It provides basic linear algebra subroutines for sparse matrices that are highly optimized for AMD GPUs.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5418ff43-3276-3c78-a76c-fe62fae8f918",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions. The analysis detected that the destination and source may refer to overlapping memory through data flow, which can cause unpredictable results when using memory copy operations like memcpy. Additionally, there are no checks ensuring that the source and destination are non-null, increasing the risk of null pointer dereference. The absence of explicit bounds or capacity checks further increases the risk. These factors collectively indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed to prevent undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory between potentially overlapping regions, use a function that safely handles overlap, such as `memmove` instead of `memcpy`. Additionally, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are non-null before performing the operation. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (val.data() != nullptr && bsr_val_C != nullptr) {\n    memmove(val.data(), bsr_val_C, sizeof(T) * block_dim * block_dim * nnzb);\n}\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination overlap, and avoids null pointer dereference. Always validate the size calculation to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "5bfbbbb0-2bb4-3a75-9101-9e421ea9bd64",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the memory copy operation. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, as indicated by the dataflow analysis. Additionally, there is no evidence of a guard to ensure the source is non-null, which increases the risk of a null pointer dereference. The destination is confirmed to be non-null, but the source is not. The use of a generic count expression (not directly tied to the destination's capacity) further increases the risk. These factors together indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that could lead to undefined behavior or program crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory, ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap. If overlap is possible, use `memmove` instead of `memcpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping regions safely. Additionally, check that the source pointer is not null before performing the copy to avoid null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (bsr_col_ind_C != nullptr && col.data() != nullptr) {\n    memmove(col.data(), bsr_col_ind_C, sizeof(J) * nnzb);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5a093de-6765-3da4-af4d-b616f593aaeb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `atoi()` function is used, which can lead to integer overflow if the input string represents a number larger than `INT_MAX`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking or input validation. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For instance, instead of `atoi()`, consider using `std::stoi()`, which throws an exception if the input string cannot be converted to an integer or if the converted value is out of the range of representable values by an `int`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi()` function with `std::stoi()`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\ntry {\n    r_level = std::stoi(argv[++i]);\n} catch (const std::invalid_argument& ia) {\n    // Handle the exception\n} catch (const std::out_of_range& oor) {\n    // Handle the exception\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<string>`: for `std::stoi()`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `std::invalid_argument` and `std::out_of_range`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
