            {
              "id": "f7c3bf3b-0754-364a-a57e-10e346de51d4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In the case of file operations, C++ provides a safer alternative in the form of file stream objects (`fstream`). These objects can handle errors more gracefully and are less prone to security vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with the `ifstream` object from the `fstream` library. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ifstream f(this->m_filename.c_str());\nif (!f.is_open()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `fstream`: for file operations\n- `string`: for string operations\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d03ef344-312a-38cb-9c9a-40fd0ec1be90",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, instead of `fopen`, use the file stream objects provided by the C++ Standard Library, such as `std::ifstream` for reading files.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `fopen` function with `std::ifstream`. Here is the fixed code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::ifstream file(this->m_filename);\nif (!file) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for `std::ifstream`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "f60145fb-5109-348e-9d8d-125e0b783746",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the filename is not properly validated or controlled.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to implement proper checks and controls to prevent misuse. For the `fopen` function, it is important to ensure that the filename is properly validated and that it does not contain any user-controlled input without proper sanitization and validation. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::ofstream file;\nfile.open(filename_, std::ios::out | std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) {\n    // handle error\n}\nh->f = &file;\n```\n\nIn this fix, the C++ `std::ofstream` class is used instead of the `fopen` function. This class provides a safer and more flexible interface for file operations.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for the `std::ofstream` class.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0f78ce35-527d-3878-ba87-053b9a22404f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the filename is manipulated by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions. In this case, instead of using `fopen`, you can use `fopen_s` which is a safer version of `fopen`. It's also important to validate and sanitize all inputs, especially those that are used in file operations.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nerrno_t err;\nerr = fopen_s(&(h->f), filename_, \"rb\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdio.h` for `fopen_s` function\n- `errno.h` for `errno_t` type\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are active and accessible for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
