            {
              "id": "2849d65c-2334-3f4c-95b7-a462a2550b04",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which is a standard C library function that converts a string to an integer. The problem with `atoi()` is that it does not handle errors well. If the input string cannot be converted into an integer, it returns zero, which can lead to unexpected behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `atoi()`. Instead, use functions that can handle errors better, such as `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()` in C++. These functions provide mechanisms to check if the entire string was converted, if any characters were left over, or if the converted value was out of range.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `atoi()` with `strtol()`, `sscanf()`, or `stoi()`:\n\n```cpp\nchar *end;\nlong int trials = strtol(argv[2], &end, 10);\nif (end == argv[2] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `stdlib.h` for `strtol()`\n- `errno.h` for `errno`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
