            {
              "id": "6c0db8bc-d44e-3cfd-b90e-320c66db5fc6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The source and destination expressions both reference elements of the same array or object, which increases the likelihood of memory overlap. The function used for copying does not guarantee correct behavior when the source and destination overlap, and this can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and there is no evidence that the source or destination pointers are null at this point, which means the operation will proceed and may trigger undefined behavior. The risk is further highlighted by the classification and the verdict, both indicating a high-confidence issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between potentially overlapping memory regions, use a memory-safe copy function that supports overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. Replace the string copy operation with a call to `memmove`, ensuring that the size argument matches the intended number of bytes to copy. For example:\n\n```cpp\nmemmove(this->argv[j + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n```\n\nThis change ensures that the copy operation is safe even if the source and destination regions overlap, as `memmove` is specifically designed to handle such cases. Always verify that the size argument does not exceed the capacity of the destination buffer to avoid buffer overflows.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
