            {
              "id": "a4aff05a-748d-3b61-b3b7-bad0fc90e885",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. The analysis detected that both the source and destination expressions are derived from the same base object, which increases the risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions. The function used for copying does not guarantee correct behavior when the source and destination overlap, which can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination buffer's capacity is not clearly defined or enforced. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can further increase the risk of string-related vulnerabilities. The source and destination are both confirmed to be non-null, so null pointer dereference is not a concern here. The main risk is the undefined behavior from overlapping memory regions during the copy operation.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying strings where the source and destination may overlap, use a memory-safe copy function that supports overlapping regions. In C/C++, `memmove` is designed for this purpose. Replace the string copy operation with a memory move operation, ensuring you copy the correct number of bytes and handle null-termination if necessary. For example:\n\n```cpp\n// Calculate the number of bytes to copy, ensuring it does not exceed the buffer size\nsize_t bytes_to_copy = rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN;\nmemmove(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], bytes_to_copy);\n// If the destination is meant to be a string, ensure null-termination\nthis->argv[k + i][bytes_to_copy - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that even if the source and destination overlap, the copy will be performed safely. Always verify that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data plus the null terminator if working with strings.\n\n## References\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP: Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "829af352-df29-3442-b29b-31fc0df6356c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy function where the source and destination buffers may overlap in memory. The analysis detected that both the source and destination expressions are derived from the same base object, which increases the risk of undefined behavior due to overlapping memory regions. The function used for copying does not guarantee correct behavior when the source and destination overlap, which can lead to data corruption or unpredictable program behavior. There are no explicit checks or guards in the code to prevent this overlap, and the destination buffer's size is not explicitly known or enforced. The source and destination are both confirmed to be non-null, but the overlap risk remains the primary concern.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying between potentially overlapping memory regions, use a memory-safe function that supports overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. `memmove` is specifically designed to handle overlapping source and destination buffers safely. Replace the string copy operation with a call to `memmove`, ensuring you copy the correct number of bytes and handle null-termination if necessary.\n\n```cpp\n// Replace:\n// strncpy(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n\n// With:\nmemmove(this->argv[k + i], this->argv[k + 1 + i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n// If the destination buffer is used as a string, ensure it is null-terminated after memmove:\nthis->argv[k + i][rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "552f0a74-a0fe-3bf1-b664-daef24481c68",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the call to the string copy function. The destination and source may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed by the standard for this function and can result in unpredictable program behavior. Additionally, there is a possibility that either the source or destination pointer could be null, as there are no clear checks or guards in place to prevent this. The use of a constant maximum length for the copy does not mitigate the risk of overlap or null pointer dereference. These factors indicate a real and actionable issue that should be addressed to ensure program correctness and security.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent undefined behavior caused by overlapping memory regions, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap before performing the copy. If there is any chance of overlap, use a memory-safe function or explicitly check for overlap and handle accordingly. Additionally, add checks to ensure that neither the source nor the destination pointer is null before calling the copy function. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->argv[i] != nullptr && argv_[i] != nullptr && this->argv[i] != argv_[i]) {\n    strncpy(this->argv[i], argv_[i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n    this->argv[i][rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nIf overlap is possible and unavoidable, consider using `memmove` instead of `strncpy`, as it is defined for overlapping memory regions:\n\n```cpp\nif (this->argv[i] != nullptr && argv_[i] != nullptr) {\n    memmove(this->argv[i], argv_[i], (size_t)rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN);\n    this->argv[i][rocsparseio_cmdline_t::MAXLEN - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS111237"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e8fba3b7-ce29-3c26-9e5e-73e9997bc35b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the input is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or ensure proper input validation and error handling. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to validate the `ifilename` input to ensure it does not contain any malicious values that could lead to issues such as path traversal attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::string ifilename;\n// Ensure ifilename is properly validated here\nstd::ifstream f(ifilename);\nif (!f.is_open()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we use the `std::ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library instead of `fopen`. This class provides a higher level of abstraction and is generally safer to use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This is a standard C++ library for input/output stream operations on files.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "df7f6471-66fa-3b2b-8f9e-26f6c28448de",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of several variables (as in the provided code snippet), there is a risk of integer overflow. This can lead to the allocation of less memory than expected, which in turn can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication with the maximum value that can be held by the type of the variable used to store the result. If the result is greater, it means that an overflow has occurred and the program should handle this situation appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t size = ...; // size of each element\nsize_t nnzb = ...; // number of elements\nsize_t bm = ...; // multiplier 1\nsize_t bn = ...; // multiplier 2\n\nif (nnzb > 0 && bm > 0 && bn > 0 && SIZE_MAX / nnzb / bm / bn < size) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g. by terminating the program\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(size * nnzb * bm * bn);\nif (!ptr) {\n    // handle failed allocation\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n\n// ...\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `climits` for `SIZE_MAX`\n- `cstdlib` for `malloc` and `exit`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ec38f59f-63b2-31cb-96b2-ac51d1afb885",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size * nnzb)` function in C++ is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The vulnerability arises when the multiplication `size * nnzb` results in an integer overflow, which can lead to the allocation of less memory than expected. This can further lead to buffer overflow attacks when more data than allocated is written to the memory block.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check for integer overflows when performing arithmetic operations for memory allocation. Also, always validate the input used in such operations to ensure they are within expected ranges.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nif (nnzb > 0 && size > SIZE_MAX / nnzb) {\n    /* Handle error: multiplication would overflow */\n} else {\n    void *ptr = malloc(size * nnzb);\n    if (!ptr) {\n        /* Handle error: allocation failed */\n    }\n    /* Continue with program, free memory when done */\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would result in an overflow. If it would, we handle the error appropriately. If not, we proceed with the allocation.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "81f29974-93e1-3ba5-9189-47937d604af5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in this case is related to the `malloc` function in C++. The `malloc` function is used to allocate a block of memory on the heap. The size of the memory block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a calculation, and the calculation results in an integer overflow, then the allocated memory block will be smaller than expected. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, where data can be written past the end of the allocated block, potentially leading to code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`.\n2. Always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful.\n3. Consider using functions that automatically handle memory allocation size calculations and overflow checking, such as `calloc`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (size && ob > SIZE_MAX / size) {\n    /* Handle overflow */\n    return NULL;\n}\n\nvoid *ptr = malloc(size * (ob + 1));\nif (!ptr) {\n    /* Handle failed allocation */\n    return NULL;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fdd4a902-ebce-3432-82b2-9baebd43e4f7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the multiplication of `data_type_size * m * n` results in an integer overflow. This can lead to the allocation of less memory than expected, which can further lead to buffer overflow attacks if the program attempts to write more data to the allocated memory than it can hold.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. If an overflow is detected, the program should handle it gracefully, for example by returning an error or by limiting the values of `m` and `n` so that their multiplication does not exceed the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the result.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (m > 0 && n > 0 && data_type_size > 0) {\n    if (m > INT_MAX / n / data_type_size) {\n        // handle overflow, e.g. by returning an error\n    } else {\n        void* ptr = malloc(data_type_size * m * n);\n        // continue with the program\n    }\n} else {\n    // handle invalid values of m, n, or data_type_size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "bdec2d50-63f2-3687-bbc0-59d8afaea468",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the input is not properly validated or sanitized.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or ensure proper input validation and error handling. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to validate the `ifilename` input to ensure it does not contain any malicious values that could lead to issues such as path traversal attacks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nstd::string ifilename;\n// Ensure ifilename is properly validated here\nstd::ifstream f(ifilename);\nif (!f.is_open()) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we use the `std::ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library instead of `fopen`. This class provides a higher level of abstraction and is generally safer to use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: This is a standard C++ library for input/output stream operations on files.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c04467b7-84d3-3570-821d-9bbe68a4eb68",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9145b226-4f1c-3d6b-94fc-7f5ea36aa453",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak random number generators or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable values and can be exploited by an attacker. The `rand()` function in C++ is a weak random number generator and is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nIn the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nt = static_cast<float>(rand()) / static_cast<float>(RAND_MAX)\n```\n\nThe `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. This function is not cryptographically secure and can produce predictable results, which can be exploited by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong, cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library, which provides facilities to generate random numbers with high quality of randomness.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nt = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "9145b226-4f1c-3d6b-94fc-7f5ea36aa453"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8035b645-9e26-33af-9572-97f440f27295",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak random number generators or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable values and can be exploited by an attacker. The `rand()` function in C++ is a weak random number generator and is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nIn the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nt = static_cast<float>(rand()) / static_cast<float>(RAND_MAX)\n```\n\nThe `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. This function is not cryptographically secure and can produce predictable results, which can be exploited by an attacker.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong, cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use the `<random>` library, which provides facilities to generate random numbers with high quality of randomness.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nt = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_real_distribution` produces random floating-point numbers in the range [0, 1).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "8035b645-9e26-33af-9572-97f440f27295"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "6fed2047-7a8d-3172-afb7-8a5d54e72618",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak random number generators or non-cryptographic random number generators in the code. These generators can produce predictable outcomes, which can be exploited by attackers to predict the sequence of random numbers and compromise the security of the system.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` function is used which is a weak random number generator. It is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context as it does not provide sufficient entropy and can be easily predicted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which is a part of the C++ Standard Library. This library provides facilities to generate random numbers with high quality of randomness.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nt = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`). `std::uniform_real_distribution` is used to generate a random double between 0 and 1.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library from the C++ Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "6fed2047-7a8d-3172-afb7-8a5d54e72618"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1d250351-7c6c-3027-aa9e-a831626df3ec",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of weak random number generators or non-cryptographic random number generators in the code. These generators can produce predictable outcomes, which can be exploited by attackers to predict the sequence of random numbers and compromise the security of the system.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` function is used which is a weak random number generator. It is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context as it does not provide sufficient entropy and can be easily predicted.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which is a part of the C++ Standard Library. This library provides facilities to generate random numbers with high quality of randomness.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the given code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\nt = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`). `std::uniform_real_distribution` is used to generate a random double between 0 and 1.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library from the C++ Standard Library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
