            {
              "id": "e0df58a4-72dc-352a-9253-0a8de5e25e98",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions also have their own issues and may not be available on all platforms. \n\nA better solution is to use C++ string objects (`std::string`), which automatically manage memory and help prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `std::string::copy` function, which checks the size of the destination buffer:\n\n```cpp\nthis->filename.copy(this->b_mlbsr.c_str(), this->b_mlbsr.size());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strcpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "7d574daf-0ed6-3c0f-b5ab-55259b6ec919",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions prevent buffer overflow by not copying more characters than the destination buffer can hold.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strncpy` function, which includes a size parameter:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_mlcsr.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "e8ae402b-1b66-36d1-bd0d-9af1c7334b58",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `snprintf`. However, these functions have their own issues and may not be available on all platforms. \n\nA better solution is to avoid using C-style strings and arrays altogether and use C++ strings and vectors, which automatically manage memory and help prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strncpy` function, which includes a size parameter:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_matrixmarket.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null termination\n```\n\nOr, better yet, use a C++ string:\n\n```cpp\nthis->filename = this->b_matrixmarket;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strcpy` and `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "8cc39051-7f69-359c-9529-e074b55091a3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause memory corruption, leading to unpredictable program behavior, crashes, or even code execution. This is a common security vulnerability in C++ programs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the source string including the null-terminating character. This can be done by using safer alternatives to `strcpy` such as `strncpy`, which takes the size of the destination buffer as an argument and ensures that no overflow occurs. However, `strncpy` does not null-terminate the destination string if the source string is larger than the buffer, so you should manually add the null-terminating character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy` and manually add the null-terminating character:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_rocalution.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions.\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class and its `c_str` method.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fe25662b-a6be-3c56-a40d-1707a6574468",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including denial of service, code execution, and information disclosure. This is because overflowing the buffer can overwrite adjacent memory, potentially altering the program's execution flow or revealing sensitive information.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter. This parameter is used to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied from the source string, preventing buffer overflow.\n\nIn C++, you can use the `strncpy` function, which includes a length parameter. However, `strncpy` does not null-terminate the destination string if the source string is longer than the specified length. Therefore, you must manually add a null terminator to ensure the destination string is properly terminated.\n\nAlternatively, you can use the `strlcpy` function, which is safer than both `strcpy` and `strncpy`. `strlcpy` copies up to the specified length from the source string to the destination string and ensures the result is null-terminated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strlcpy` function:\n\n```cpp\nstrlcpy(this->filename, this->b_rocsparseio.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename));\n```\n\nThis code will copy the string from `b_rocsparseio` to `filename`, but it will not exceed the size of `filename`, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strcpy` function.\n- `<string>`: This library provides the `std::string` class and its `c_str` method.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2501bae2-3224-3e33-a35a-73cfe54fc040",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to serious security vulnerabilities such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy` or `memcpy`. These functions require you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy` function:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_file.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nIn this code, `sizeof(this->filename) - 1` ensures that there is space for the null terminator. The last line explicitly adds the null terminator, as `strncpy` does not add it if the source is larger than or equal to the destination size.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "90fcd3d8-67a5-32e7-b34c-d7f5e1d1d75e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `std::ctime` function is used, which is not thread-safe and can lead to data races in multi-threaded programs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using non-thread-safe functions like `std::ctime`. Instead, use thread-safe alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. Always validate and sanitize input data before processing it. Be aware of the potential for buffer overflows and other common vulnerabilities when using C library functions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `std::ctime` function with a thread-safe alternative, such as `std::chrono`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <chrono>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    auto end_time = std::chrono::system_clock::now();\n    std::time_t end_time_c = std::chrono::system_clock::to_time_t(end_time);\n    std::cout << \"end time: \" << std::ctime(&end_time_c) << std::endl;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<chrono>`\n- `<ctime>`\n- `<iostream>`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0be5383e-abb8-3a45-8625-2209c8e51272",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the use of the `strlen` function in the C++ programming language. The `strlen` function is used to determine the length of a string, but it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used correctly. In the provided code snippet, the `strlen` function is used as the third argument to the `strncmp` function, which compares two strings up to a specified number of characters. If the `name` string is not null-terminated, the `strlen` function could return a value that exceeds the actual size of the `name` buffer, leading to a buffer overflow when `strncmp` is called.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that strings in C++ are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing strings with a null character at the end, or by manually adding a null character at the end of a string after it has been modified. Additionally, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that do not rely on null-termination, such as `strnlen` and `strncmp`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA safer version of the code snippet would look like this:\n\n```cpp\n0 == strncmp(name, archname, strnlen(name, sizeof(name)))\n```\n\nIn this version, the `strnlen` function is used instead of `strlen`. The `strnlen` function returns the length of the string or the maximum number of characters to consider, whichever is smaller. This prevents a buffer overflow even if the `name` string is not null-terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncmp` and `strnlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
