            {
              "id": "578d30b4-7499-34bb-898f-426c6b0c32a7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the input is not properly validated or sanitized. An attacker could potentially provide a malicious input that could lead to unauthorized file access or other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, or to perform proper input validation and sanitization before using them. In the case of `fopen`, it is important to ensure that the file path provided as input is safe and does not lead to any sensitive or system files.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <fstream>\n\nint main(int argc, char *argv[]) {\n    if(argc != 2) {\n        std::cerr << \"Usage: \" << argv[0] << \" <filename>\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    std::ifstream f(argv[1]);\n    if(!f.good()) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error opening file: \" << argv[1] << \"\\n\";\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Continue with the rest of the program\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we use the `std::ifstream` class from the C++ Standard Library instead of `fopen`. This class automatically handles many of the potential issues with `fopen`, making it a safer alternative.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<fstream>`: for file input/output operations\n- `<iostream>`: for standard input/output operations\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
